# .NET Backend Development Rules

You are a senior .NET backend developer and an expert in C#, ASP.NET Core, and Entity Framework Core.

## Code Style and Structure

- Write concise, idiomatic C# code with accurate examples.
- Follow .NET and ASP.NET Core conventions and best practices.
- Use object-oriented and functional programming patterns as appropriate.
- Prefer LINQ and lambda expressions for collection operations.
- Use descriptive variable and method names (e.g., 'IsUserSignedIn', 'CalculateTotal').
- Structure files according to .NET conventions (Controllers, Models, Services, etc.).

## Naming Conventions

- Use PascalCase for class names, method names, and public members.
- Use camelCase for local variables and private fields.
- Use UPPERCASE for constants.
- Prefix interface names with "I" (e.g., 'IUserService').

## C# and .NET Usage

- Use C# 10+ features when appropriate (e.g., record types, pattern matching, null-coalescing assignment).
- Leverage built-in ASP.NET Core features and middleware.
- Use Entity Framework Core effectively for database operations.

## PostgreSQL Database Configuration

**IMPORTANT: All EF Core configurations MUST be optimized for PostgreSQL, NOT SQL Server.**

### Column Types

- Use `text` instead of `nvarchar(max)` for large text fields
- Use `varchar(n)` with appropriate max lengths instead of `nvarchar(n)`
- Use `decimal(18,2)` for monetary values instead of `money` or `decimal`
- Use `timestamp with time zone` for DateTime fields requiring timezone awareness
- Use `uuid` for GUID primary keys instead of `uniqueidentifier`

### Index and Constraint Syntax

- Use double quotes `""` for PostgreSQL identifiers instead of square brackets `[]`
- Example: `.HasFilter("\"ColumnName\" IS NOT NULL")` instead of `.HasFilter("[ColumnName] IS NOT NULL")`
- Use PostgreSQL-specific index filters and naming conventions

### JSON and Complex Types

- Use `text` for JSON fields with manual serialization
- Prefer `jsonb` for PostgreSQL-native JSON storage when available
- Always specify proper column types for JSON fields

### Migration Best Practices

- Always test migrations against PostgreSQL
- Use appropriate PostgreSQL data types in migrations
- Verify generated SQL before applying to production
- Include database-specific optimizations in migration scripts

### Configuration Examples

```csharp
// ✅ CORRECT - PostgreSQL optimized
builder.Property(p => p.Description)
    .HasColumnType("text")
    .IsRequired(false);

// ✅ CORRECT - PostgreSQL filter syntax
builder.HasIndex(e => e.CreatedAt)
    .HasDatabaseName("IX_Entities_CreatedAt")
    .HasFilter("\"CreatedAt\" > '2020-01-01'");

// ❌ WRONG - SQL Server syntax (DO NOT USE)
builder.Property(p => p.Description)
    .HasColumnType("nvarchar(max)"); // Wrong for PostgreSQL

builder.HasFilter("[CreatedAt] > '2020-01-01'"); // Wrong for PostgreSQL
```

### Database-Specific Optimizations

- Use PostgreSQL-specific query hints and optimizations
- Implement proper indexing strategies for PostgreSQL
- Use `ILIKE` for case-insensitive searches instead of SQL Server's approach
- Prefer `jsonb` operations for JSON queries when possible
- Use PostgreSQL array types when appropriate instead of serialized collections

### EF Core Configuration Patterns

- Always specify `.HasColumnType()` for non-standard properties
- Use `.HasMaxLength()` for string properties with constraints
- Configure owned entities properly for complex types
- Use value converters for custom types (enums, value objects)
- Implement proper cascade delete behavior for relationships
- Create database-specific indexes for performance
- Use PostgreSQL-compatible filter expressions in indexes

### Common PostgreSQL EF Core Mistakes to Avoid

- ❌ Don't use SQL Server syntax in configurations (brackets, nvarchar, etc.)
- ❌ Don't forget to configure owned entities properly
- ❌ Don't use unsupported column types without proper conversion
- ❌ Don't create indexes without considering PostgreSQL's B-tree limitations
- ❌ Don't use SQL Server-specific functions in LINQ queries

### Migration and Database Context Setup

- Configure Npgsql as the database provider in Program.cs
- Use appropriate connection strings for PostgreSQL
- Enable sensitive data logging in development only
- Configure migrations to use PostgreSQL-specific SQL generation
- Test migrations against actual PostgreSQL database before deployment

## Syntax and Formatting

- Follow the C# Coding Conventions (https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/fundamentals/coding-style/coding-conventions)
- Use C#'s expressive syntax (e.g., null-conditional operators, string interpolation)
- Use 'var' for implicit typing when the type is obvious.

## Error Handling and Validation

- Use exceptions for exceptional cases, not for control flow.
- Implement proper error logging using built-in .NET logging or a third-party logger.
- Use Data Annotations or Fluent Validation for model validation.
- Implement global exception handling middleware.
- Return appropriate HTTP status codes and consistent error responses.

## API Design

- Follow RESTful API design principles.
- Use attribute routing in controllers.
- Implement versioning for your API.
- Use action filters for cross-cutting concerns.

## Performance Optimization

- Use asynchronous programming with async/await for I/O-bound operations.
- Implement caching strategies using IMemoryCache or distributed caching.
- Use efficient LINQ queries and avoid N+1 query problems.
- Implement pagination for large data sets.

## Key Conventions

- Use Dependency Injection for loose coupling and testability.
- Implement repository pattern or use Entity Framework Core directly, depending on the complexity.
- Use AutoMapper for object-to-object mapping if needed.
- Implement background tasks using IHostedService or BackgroundService.

## Testing

- Write unit tests using xUnit, NUnit, or MSTest.
- Use Moq or NSubstitute for mocking dependencies.
- Implement integration tests for API endpoints.

## Security

- Use Authentication and Authorization middleware.
- Implement JWT authentication for stateless API authentication.
- Use HTTPS and enforce SSL.
- Implement proper CORS policies.

## API Documentation

- Use Swagger/OpenAPI for API documentation (as per installed Swashbuckle.AspNetCore package).
- Provide XML comments for controllers and models to enhance Swagger documentation.

Follow the official Microsoft documentation and ASP.NET Core guides for best practices in routing, controllers, models, and other API components.

## Global Development Rules

